# 7. Scaling Challenges

This section differentiates SDE-2 candidates.

---

## Problem 1 — GPU Bottleneck

LLMs are GPU-expensive.

Solutions:

* Request batching
* Quantization
* Model sharding
* Distillation
* KV caching

---

## Problem 2 — Long Context Windows

Large conversations increase latency.

Solutions:

* Context summarization
* Sliding windows
* Semantic retrieval
* Conversation compression

---

## Problem 3 — High QPS

Millions of users simultaneously.

Solutions:

* Horizontal scaling
* Stateless APIs
* Load balancing
* Queue-based inference

---

## Problem 4 — Hot Conversations

Popular chats cause repeated reads.

Use:

* Redis caching

Cache:

* Recent conversation history
* User session state
* Embeddings

---

# 8. Explain AI Safety Layer

This is a strong differentiator.

## Moderation Pipeline

Before prompt reaches model:

* Toxicity checks
* Jailbreak detection
* Prompt injection filtering

After response:

* Safety moderation
* PII filtering
* Policy enforcement

---

# 9. Reliability & Observability

Mention this explicitly.

## Metrics

Track:

* Token latency
* Time to first token (TTFT)
* GPU utilization
* Error rate
* Queue length
* Tokens/sec

---

## Reliability Techniques

* Circuit breakers
* Retry mechanisms
* Backpressure handling
* Fallback smaller models
* Multi-region deployment

---

# 10. Cost Optimization

This is a VERY common follow-up.

LLMs are expensive.

Optimizations:

* Smaller models for easy queries
* Caching repeated prompts
* Dynamic model routing
* Quantized inference
* Autoscaling GPUs

Example:

```text
Simple math → small model
Complex coding → large model
```

---

# 11. Multi-Model Architecture

Modern AI systems rarely use ONE model.

Architecture:

```text
Router
 ├── Fast cheap model
 ├── Coding model
 ├── Reasoning model
 └── Image model
```

Routing based on:

* Query type
* Latency needs
* Cost constraints

---

# 12. Security Considerations

Mention briefly:

* Encryption at rest
* TLS in transit
* PII masking
* RBAC
* Signed URLs for uploads
* Audit logs

---

# 13. Strong Interview Closing Statement

A good wrap-up:

> “A ChatGPT-like system is essentially a massively distributed real-time inference platform combining API orchestration, distributed storage, GPU scheduling, semantic retrieval, and streaming token generation while balancing latency, scalability, safety, and cost.”

That sounds senior-level.

